    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Göktürk Empire was born from an insult. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a>, crushed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uyghurs">Uyghur</a> revolt in 546 CE and expected a Rouran princess as reward. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khagan">khagan</a> refused, calling him a blacksmith slave. Bumin allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#western-wei">Western Wei</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, married a Wei princess, and in 552 CE destroyed the Rouran in a decisive battle, declaring himself Qaghan and becoming the first leader to use Türk as a political name. He died within months. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muqan-qaghan">Muqan Qaghan</a> (553–572), expansion was extraordinary: the remaining Rouran were exterminated, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khitans">Khitans</a> defeated eastward, and Muqan's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#istami">Istämi</a> drove westward past the Caspian into former Hephthalite territory. By 568 CE, the Göktürks sent their first embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>, proposing alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Khaganate</a> extracted 100,000 rolls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> annually from the Northern Zhou dynasty. By 576 CE, Göktürk forces had crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crimea">Crimea</a>, the furthest westward reach of any Turkic power before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a>. The combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-turkic-khaganate">First Khaganate</a> at its peak was likely the largest contiguous territorial entity on earth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Göktürk economy rested on tribute extraction and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> management. Sogdian merchant partners handled long-distance commercial logistics under Göktürk military protection, making the khaganate indispensable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">Silk</a> Road commerce without requiring nomadic Göktürks to administer markets directly. Tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> was the single most valuable income stream. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Khaganate</a> extracted silk, grain, and manufactured goods from successive Chinese dynasties in a relationship the Chinese framed as gifts but which functioned as tribute paid to avoid military devastation. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bilge-qaghan">Bilge Qaghan</a>, a single diplomatic exchange with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-xuanzong-of-tang">Tang Emperor Xuanzong</a> produced 100,000 bolts of silk in return for thirty horses, a ratio that illustrates the extraction mechanism precisely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pastoralism">Pastoralism</a> remained the economic base of the tribal population, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#horse-breeding">horse breeding</a> and seasonal migration sustaining the military manpower that made all other income streams possible.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Göktürk military power rested on mobile cavalry capable of rapid strategic concentration across vast steppe distances. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-turkic-khaganate">First Khaganate</a> simultaneously extracted tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>, Qi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sui-dynasty">Sui</a> dynasties, demonstrating credible military threat on multiple fronts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qapaghan-qaghan">Qapaghan Qaghan</a> (691–716), the peak military commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-turkic-khaganate">Second Khaganate</a>, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yellow-river">Yellow River</a> six times between 693 and 710 CE, forcing Tang tribute payments. A Tang army of 80,000 was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ming-sha">Battle of Ming Sha</a> by a significantly smaller Göktürk force. Qapaghan conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yenisei-kyrgyz">Yenisei Kyrgyz</a> and killed their qaghan in personal combat in 710, extending influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manchuria">Manchuria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. The empire's central military vulnerability was institutional: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lateral-succession">lateral succession</a>, passing power among brothers before sons, guaranteed a crisis at every transition. The Sui dynasty's Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#su-wei">Su Wei</a> documented the Chinese strategy explicitly: set the barbarians against each other, never let them combine.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Göktürk state was a tribal confederation organised around the supremacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a>, whose divine mandate was expressed through the concept of heaven-granted qut, or royal fortune. The qaghan commanded subordinate tribal leaders who owed military service and tribute in exchange for protection and a share of extracted wealth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-turkic-khaganate">Second Khaganate</a> produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon Inscriptions</a>, erected between 732 and 735 CE, the earliest substantial written records in any Turkic language. These monuments are simultaneously a governance document, a legitimacy claim, and a warning against Chinese cultural assimilation, condemning Turkic nobles who had adopted Tang titles as traitors. The inscriptions reveal a regime acutely aware of its own fragility, using permanent stone monuments to enforce political loyalty that military power alone could not guarantee. Administration beyond the Mongolian heartland was exercised through subordinate tribal leaders rather than appointed governors, a structural limitation that made the empire's cohesion entirely dependent on the personal authority of the reigning qaghan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Göktürk Empire's significance extends far beyond its 192-year lifespan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-turkic-khaganate">First Khaganate</a> established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a>'s greatest period of security, enabling movement of goods, religions, and ideas between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> under a single protective military umbrella. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon script</a>, the first written Turkic language, created a literary tradition that influenced subsequent steppe civilisations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a>'s confederation model was inherited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uyghurs">Uyghur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgars">Bulgar</a> khaganates, and the broader Turkic political tradition eventually flowed into the Seljuk and Ottoman empires. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gokturk-embassy-to-constantinople">568 CE embassy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> established a direct diplomatic relationship between the steppe world and Byzantium that shaped Byzantine foreign policy for generations. The Orkhon Inscriptions remain the foundational texts of Turkic literary heritage, studied today as the earliest window into Turkic political thought.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-turkic-khaganate">First Khaganate</a> collapsed in 603 CE through the mechanism its enemies had cultivated: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lateral-succession">lateral succession</a> produced simultaneous rival claimants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sui-dynasty">Sui</a> exploited the divisions, and the unified empire fragmented. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Khaganate</a> was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in 630 CE, its qaghan captured in a surprise night attack by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#li-jing">Li Jing</a>. The Western Khaganate fell in 657 CE. Between 679 and 681, two revolts failed and fifty-four Göktürk nobles were publicly executed in the eastern market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changan">Chang'an</a>. The resurrection came in 682 CE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilterish-qaghan">Ilterish Qaghan</a> began with seventeen followers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yin-shan">Yin Shan</a> mountains and rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-turkic-khaganate">Second Khaganate</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, peaking under Qapaghan and consolidated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bilge-qaghan">Bilge</a>. The deaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tonyukuk">Tonyukuk</a> in 726, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kul-tigin">Kül Tigin</a> in 731, and Bilge himself in 734, poisoned by his own chancellor, removed the entire governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cohort">cohort</a> within eight years. In 744 CE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karluks">Karluks</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basmyls">Basmyls</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#otuken">Ötüken</a>, beheaded the last Göktürk qaghan, and sent his head to the Tang court.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Göktürk Empire's enduring significance lies in its double destruction and double resurrection: a nomadic people with no cities, no agriculture, and no written language built the largest empire on earth, lost it entirely to foreign conquest, rebuilt it from seventeen men, and left a civilisational legacy that shaped every major Turkic state for the next thousand years.</em></p>

